{
 "_comment": [
  "The atlas of overhead line supports that stand along a German railway line.",
  "One entry per distinguishable silhouette, not per manufacturer's drawing — a",
  "route needs the shapes a passenger can tell apart from a moving train, and",
  "that is the mast picture (Mastbild), the material and the height.",
  "",
  "See README.md for the sources behind every number and for the licence survey",
  "of the models the geometry can come from.",
  "",
  "id: kebab-case, `<shape>-<voltage>`; the file stem a track object would use.",
  "name_de / name_en: the German term is the real one — `Donaumast` is what the",
  "  drawings say. The English name is descriptive and exists for the atlas only;",
  "  user-visible text goes through i18n, never through this file.",
  "category: transmission | distribution | railway-power | telecom",
  "osm: the OpenStreetMap tags this type is mapped with. `design` is the key that",
  "  carries the silhouette (wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Key:design) — the route",
  "  editor's Overpass import can pick the type straight from it.",
  "voltage_kv / frequency_hz: 50 Hz for the public grid, 16.7 Hz for Bahnstrom.",
  "circuits: three-phase systems on the mast; a Bahnstrom circuit is two-pole.",
  "conductors: live conductors in total, earth wires excluded.",
  "earth_wires: 0, 1 or 2 above the topmost crossarm.",
  "structure: steel-lattice | steel-tube | spun-concrete | wood",
  "height_m: [min, max] over ground, to the mast tip including the earth peak.",
  "crossarms: top to bottom. `width_m` is the full width tip to tip and",
  "  `conductors` counts both sides together; `at_frac` is the lower chord's",
  "  conductor height as a share of the mast's, and `depth_m` is the vertical",
  "  truss depth from that chord to the upper root joint at the body.",
  "span_m: [min, max] distance to the next mast on level ground.",
  "roles: which functions this shape is built in — suspension (Tragmast),",
  "  tension (Abspannmast), angle (Winkelmast), terminal (Endmast),",
  "  branch (Abzweigmast), transposition (Verdrillungsmast).",
  "region: nationwide | west | east | rare — where in Germany it dominates.",
  "epochs: German model railway epochs the shape belongs to (III = 1945-1970,",
  "  IV = 1970-1990, V = 1990-2006, VI = 2007-). Feeds the `epoch-*` object tags.",
  "confidence: `sourced` when every number in the entry is backed by a citation",
  "  in README.md, `estimated` when the shape is documented but the dimensions",
  "  are read off photographs and the neighbouring sourced types.",
  "tags: what a route editor content drawer filters on.",
  "build: what the generator needs beyond the shape (tools/pylons/lib/kit.mjs).",
  "  kind: lattice | pole | tube | portal — which body is assembled.",
  "  base_m / shaft_m: the square (or the diameter) at the foot and at the top.",
  "  waist: knee where the steep lower batter changes to the shallow upper",
  "    taper, as a share of the height; 1.0 for one continuous taper.",
  "  panels / shaft_panels: bracing panels below and above the waist.",
  "  leg_m / brace_m / arm_m: member sizes.",
  "  peak_m / peaks: the earth-wire peak's height and how many there are.",
  "  fitting: suspension | composite | pin | bell — what hangs or stands at a",
  "    conductor. `composite` is a grey silicone long rod, not a cap chain.",
  "  insulator_m plus insulator_discs or insulator_sheds: the fitting's length",
  "    and repeating units. Follows the **fitting**, not the body: a 20 kV",
  "    lattice pole carries",
  "    the same 35 cm pin insulator a concrete pole does."
 ],
 "finish": {
  "_comment": [
   "How the steel looks. Nothing here is per type — it applies to every lattice",
   "mast in the atlas and belongs in the material, not in the geometry."
  ],
  "steel_lattice": "hot-dip galvanised, weathering from bright metallic grey to a flat dull grey over about ten years; no paint",
  "aviation_marking": "red/white banding on the top third and orange marker balls (Warnkugeln, 60 cm) on the earth wire near crossings and airfields",
  "insulators": "long rod or cap-and-pin strings, porcelain brown or glass green on old lines, grey silicone composite on anything built after about 1995",
  "signage": "a yellow-and-black lightning arrow plate and the mast number stencilled about 3 m above the foot",
  "bird_protection": "black-and-white bird deflectors on the earth wire, insulator hoods on medium voltage — mandatory on new lines since the 2002 amendment of the BNatSchG"
 },
